      "nets": ["NET0123", "NET0072", "+3.3V", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the FCC Peripherals sheet all six PCA9685PW address pins (A0–A5, pins 1,2,3,4,24,25) are tied to GND, which sets its 7-bit I2C address to 0x40. The PWM driver's SCL/SDA are on NET0123/NET0072, the same nets used by the INA219AIDCNR (U2, pin5 SCL=NET0123, pin6 SDA=NET0072) whose address pins A0/A1 are also at GND (0x40). Thus two devices on the same bus share address 0x40.",
      "expected": "The schematic's own annotation for the PWM driver states 'I2C address: 0x41', which requires A0 tied to +3.3V (VDD). With the INA219 fixed at 0x40 on the same bus, the PCA9685 must be at 0x41 (or another non-colliding address) for the I2C bus to function.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "FCC Peripherals sheet, U19 PCA9685PW: 'I2C address: 0x41' with pins A0–A5 drawn to GND symbols"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet, U2 INA219AIDCNR: 'I2C address: 0x40', SCL=NET0123, SDA=NET0072"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "FCC Peripherals sheet, U19 SCL=NET0123, SDA=NET0072 (same bus as INA219)"}
      ],
      "impact": "Bus contention / address collision: both the current-voltage sensor and the 16-channel PWM/servo driver answer to 0x40 on the same I2C bus. The MCU cannot reliably address either device, so servo/PWM outputs and the battery current/voltage reading would behave incorrectly or not respond.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build appears to deviate in the PCA9685PW I2C address selection: all address pins are tied to GND (0x40) even though the schematic note and the need to avoid a collision with the INA219 (also 0x40 on the same NET0123/NET0072 bus) call for 0x41, producing an I2C address conflict. Confidence is moderate because the design-spec component table lists the PCA9685 as 0x40, which conflicts with the drawing's own 0x41 note."
}

